#.(Begin_Help_File)
#.($3)Configure
#The \bConfigure\r pull-down menu allows you to set up and customize
#various aspects of the operation of the emulator hardware and
#the host software.
#.($4)Emulator
#The \bEmulator\r command invokes a pull-down menu to allow you to
#establish the operational characteristics of the emulator base
#and the probe card (pod).  These include such things as
#the \l($17)communication^port\m
#and \l($20)baud^rate\m
#to be used for the RS-232 communication
#link between the host PC and the emulator base.
#.($5)Execute
#The \bExecute\r command first establishes communication between
#the host software system and the emulator base unit.
#Then, the host software initializes the emulator base.
#This initialization usually only needs to be performed once
#at the beginning of each session.
#.sp 1
#The host software uses the current values associated with
#\l($17)Comm^port\m and \l($20)Baud^rate\m
#to establish communication and initialize the emulator base.
#When you change any of these values, all are written to
#the \l($250)$CONFIG\m file.  The host software automatically reads
#this file when it begins executing.  Thus, if you change any
#of these values, the new settings become your defaults.
#.($6)Mode
#The \bMode\r command allows you to specify the probe card's mode
#of operation.  This command is available (active) only for those probe
#cards which can operate in different modes.  The available modes are
#different for each probe card. Typically, these modes consist of
#combinations of:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +3 {
#.ti -3
#-^^ROM mode / ROMless mode
#.ti -3
#-^^WDT (Watchdog Timer) On / WDT Off
#.in -3 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#Determine how the probe card should operate, based on your
#application and your target system environment, and then select
#the appropriate mode.
#.sp 1
#Note that if you change the mode of operation, the host software
#updates (or creates) the \l($250)$CONFIG\m file automatically.
#However, the actual mode of operation of the device does not change
#until you select the \l($5)Execute\m command.
#.($17)Comm port
#The \bComm^port\r command allows to select the RS-232 communication
#port on your computer which the host software will use to communicate
#with the emulator base.  The available communication ports are COM1
#and COM2.
#.($20)Baud rate
#The \bBaud^rate\r command allows you to select the baud rate
#that the host software will use during communication with the
#emulator base.  Normally, you should choose the highest (fastest)
#available baud rate to maximize system throughput.
#However, you may need to select a lower (slower) baud rate if:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +3 {
#.ti -3
#-^^the emulator base is a long distance from your computer
#.ti -3
#-^^the 8250 UART chip in your computer cannot sustain
#high-speed RS-232 communications
#.in -3 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#After establishing communication with the emulator base,
#the baud rate may not be changed
#unless the emulator is reset.
#.sp 1
#The host software establishes the baud rate you specify here
#independently of any baud rate you might have set from DOS.
#.sp 1
#Note that if you change the baud rate, the host software
#updates (or creates) the \l($250)$CONFIG\m file automatically.
#However, the actual baud rate used in communicating with the emulator
#does not change until you select the \l($5)Execute\m command.
#.($30)Code Memory Mapping
#The \bCode^Memory\r
#command invokes a menu allowing you to map
#code
#memory either to memory supplied in the emulator base or to memory
#(which you must provide) in your target system.
#.sp 1
#The host software downloads (to the emulator) changes in the map
#settings on exit from the
#Code
#Memory Mapping menu.
#.sp 1
#Note that there are some mapping constraints due to emulator and/or
#device dependencies
#(e.g., in ROM mode, all ROM space must be mapped to the emulator base).
#The host software enforces these mapping constraints, if any.
#Since these constraints are usually dependent on the
#operating \l($6)mode\m, this menu also displays the device's current
#operating mode, if applicable.
#.($31)Map Code Block To Emulator
#The \bEmulator\r command allows you to map blocks of
#code
#memory to the
#emulator base.  The software prompts you for the starting and ending
#addresses of the range you wish to map to the emulator base.
#.($32)Map Code Block To Target
#The \bTarget\r command allows you to map blocks of
#code
#memory to your
#target board.  The software prompts you for the starting and ending
#addresses of the range you wish to map to your target board.
#.($33)Map All Code Memory
#The \bAll\r command quickly maps all of
#code
#memory to either
#the emulator base or your target board.  After selecting the \bAll\r
#command, the host software displays a pull-down menu with the two
#choices \l($34)Emulator\m and \l($35)Target\m available.
#The host software automatically enforces all mapping constraints,
#if applicable.
#.($34)Map All Code Memory To Emulator
#The \bEmulator\r command quickly maps all
#code
#memory to the emulator base.
#The host software automatically enforces all mapping constraints,
#if applicable.
#.($35)Map All Code Memory To Target
#The \bTarget\r command quickly maps all of
#code
#memory to the target board.
#The host software automatically enforces all mapping constraints,
#if applicable.
#.($36)Load Code Memory Mapping
#The \bLoad\r command reads (and displays)
#code
#memory mapping information from a file previously
#\l($37)Save\md using the \l($37)Save\m command.
#.($37)Save Code Memory Mapping
#The \bSave\r command saves (writes) the current
#code
#memory mapping information into a file.  At a later time, you can
#restore the mapping to this state by using
#the \l($36)Load\m command.
#.($42)Map All Xdata Memory | Emulator
#.($46)Display Attributes
#The display \bAttributes\r command invokes a pull-down menu which
#allows you to customize the characteristics of the video display
#on your host PC.
#.($47)Lines
#The \bLines\r command allows you change the total number of
#lines displayed on your screen.
#The choices available are dependent on the
#video display adapter installed in your host PC:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.ba {
#MDA (Monochrome Display  Adapter):   25 lines
#.sp 1
#CGA (Color      Graphics Adapter):   25 lines
#.sp 1
#EGA (Enhanced   Graphics Adapter):   25 lines
#                                     43 lines
#.sp 1
#VGA (Video      Graphics Array  ):   25 lines
#                                     28 lines
#                                     50 lines
#.ea }
#.in -4 }
#.sp 1
#Note that if you specify the \l($249)-v\m command line option,
#the host software deactivates this (\bLines\r) command selection.
#.($48)
#The 25-line display mode is the default and is available
#when using any video adapter or monitor in your PC.
#.($49)
#The 28-line display mode is available only when certain video
#adapters are installed in your PC.  See \l($47)Lines\m for details.
#.($50)
#The 43-line display mode is available only when certain video
#adapters are installed in your PC.  See \l($47)Lines\m for details.
#.($51)
#The 50-line display mode is available only when certain video
#adapters are installed in your PC.  See \l($47)Lines\m for details.
#.($53)CGA/EGA/VGA Default
#The \bCGA/EGA/VGA Default\r command sets up video colors and other
#display attributes suitable for all color monitor types.
#The supplied binary file \l($250)$CLR1$\m contains these color definitions.
#These definitions make use of 16 foreground colors,
#but only 8 background colors.  If you have an EGA or VGA installed
#in your system, the \l($54)EGA/VGA^Default\m provides a full
#16 background colors.
#.($54)EGA/VGA Default
#The \bEGA/VGA Default\r command sets up video colors and other
#display attributes suitable for EGA & VGA color monitors.
#The supplied binary file \l($250)$CLR2$\m contains these color definitions.
#These definitions make full use of 16 foreground \uand\r 16 background
#colors.  If you accidentally (or intentionally) select this command
#when the display adapter installed in your PC is actually a CGA
#or Monochrome adapter, many areas of the screen will blink!
#This is because the $CLR2$ file is set up assuming that the
#video display attribute control bit for "blink" actually means
#"bold (or bright) background".
#.($55)Monochrome Default
#The \bMonochrome Default\r command sets up video
#display attributes suitable for monochrome adapters/monitors.
#The supplied binary file \l($250)$CLRM$\m contains these color definitions.
#The only "color", other than black & white, available
#for monochrome adapters is blue.  However, "blue" does not display
#as blue, but rather as \uunderline\ring.
#If you accidentally (or intentionally) select this command
#when the display adapter installed in your PC is actually
#something other than a Monochrome adapter, the video display
#indeed \uwill\r be painted in a rather hideous combination
#of black, white and blue.
#.($56)User
#The \bUser\r command sets up video colors and other
#display attributes to your customized settings.
#The binary file \l($250)$COLOR\m (not supplied) contains these color
#definitions.
#.sp 1
#To create your own customized, default color definitions, first
#invoke the \l($57)Select\m command to change individual display
#attribute settings.  Then, \l($76)Save\m those settings into
#the $COLOR file, making sure that the \bSave Option\r
#for \l($79)Colors\m is \uon\r.
#.sp 1
#The \b%rs1\r host software reads the $COLOR file during initialization,
#thus establishing your own customized, default color definitions.
#.($57)Select
#The \bSelect\r command pops up a menu which allows you to
#select/modify video colors and other display attributes for all
#fields in all windows and menus individually.
#See \l($345)Select^Color\m for detailed information about the fields
#that you can modify in this menu.
#.($58)Configure|Windows
#The \bWindows\r command invokes a pop-menu allowing you to customize
#certain characteristics of the windows on the main screen.
#.sp 1
#The data in each of the windows is updated after every emulation
#cycle (\l($96)Run\m command) or whenever you change the value in a
#register, memory location or variable.
#.sp 1
#We recommend that you first \l($59)Modify\m the windows to:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1)^^Specify which windows will be active (present) or
#inactive (absent).
#.ti -4
#2)^^Determine the order in which the windows will be
#displayed (top of screen {1} to bottom of screen {n}).
#.in -4 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#and then \l($60)Size\m the windows to suit your needs.
#.($59)Configure|Windows|Modify
#The \bModify\r command allows you to define the layout and content
#of all the windows on the main screen.
#Use the arrow keys to move the highlighted cursor to the attribute
#you wish to change and then follow the directions displayed
#on the bottom border of the box.
#.sp 1
#The window attributes which you can change in this menu are:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +5 {
#.ti -5
#\bActive\r
#.sp 1
#Each window can either be active (present, \bYes\r)
#or inactive (absent, \bNo\r).  By default, the only active windows
#are the Register, Stack, Source and Status Windows.  And yes, you can
#operate the \b%rs1\r system with all the windows inactive.
#.sp 1
#.ti -5
#\bOrder\r
#.sp 1
#The order of presentation of the window names in
#the \bWindow\r & \bOrder\r columns mirrors the order in which the
#windows will appear on the main screen (top to bottom).
#.sp 1
#.ti -5
#\bStarting^Address\r
#.sp 1
#The Source Window always reflects the source lines and/or machine
#instructions in the vicinity of the value in the Program Counter
#register (@PC).
#.sp 1
#For most of the "dump" style windows (all except the Stack Window),
#you can choose whether the starting address for the data
#displayed in the window will be fixed (\bFixed\r), or varying based
#on the value of an expression that you specify (\b@Exp\r).
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.ti -2
#\bFixed\r
#.sp 1
#You can select a fixed starting address by pressing the
#Enter key when the cursor is positioned in the leftmost column under
#the "Starting Address" heading.
#Note that even if you choose to have a fixed starting address for a
#window, you can still associate a "Current Position" expression with
#that window (see \bCurrent Position\r below).
#.sp 1
#.ti -2
#\b@Exp\r
#.sp 1
#You can select the \b@Exp\r ("at-expression" or "@<expression>") style
#of window painting by pressing the Enter key when the cursor is
#positioned in the rightmost column (over '@Exp') under the
#"Starting Address" heading.  See \bCurrent Position\r below for
#details on specifying the current-position expression.
#.sp 1
#Any "dump" style window which has associated with it a
#current-position expression can be set up for
#painting "@<expression>".  What this means is that the host software
#will dynamically and
#continually adjust the window's starting address to ensure that
#the location designated by the expression is always visible in
#the window.  An example of this kind of window painting is
#the Stack Window (@SP).
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#As long as the location designated by the current-position expression
#is within the
#range of addresses visible in the window, the host software does not
#change the window's starting address, just the location of the
#current-position indicator character (typically, an arrow).
#As soon as the current-expression value goes "out of the window"
#(in either direction), the host software automatically changes the
#window's starting address so that the byte which is '@<expression>'
#is exactly in the middle of the window.  When the host software does
#change the window's starting address in this way, the software also
#highlights the entire address column in the window to alert you that
#the window has been "re-ORG"ed.
#If you \l($61)goto/peruse/browse\m the window
#and scroll the data in the window, the host software will leave the
#data in the window positioned as you last left it as long as the
#location which is '@<expression>' is still visible in the window.
#.in -2 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#.ti -5
#\bCurrent^Position\r
#.sp 1
#Any "dump" style window can have associated with it an arbitrary
#"Current Position" expression
#(e.g., SP or B or X).
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +7 {
#.ti -7
#NOTE:^^The current-position expression associated with the Stack Window
#is always "SP";  you cannot change the current-position expression
#associated with the Stack Window.
#.in -7 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#A current-position indicator character (e.g., an arrow --
#see \bCurrent Position:^^Indicator\r below) appears next to the
#corresponding data byte/bit if the location designated by the
#current-position expression is within the range of locations being
#displayed in the window.  You have control over the character used for
#the current-position marker, and whether it appears to the left
#or right of the corresponding data byte/bit.
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +2 {
#.ti -2
#\bCurrent^Position:^^Expression\r
#.sp 1
#If the window has a current-position expression associated with it,
#that expression appears here preceded by an at-sign (@).
#If the window does not have now have a current-position expression
#associated with it, this field contains "@---------".
#If the window \ucannot\r have a current-position expression associated
#with it, this field contains "----------".
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#To change the current-position expression associated with a window,
#follow the instructions at the bottom of the menu.  When you enter
#a new expression, do \unot\r include the leading at-sign (@) in what
#you type.
#.sp 1
#To clear (erase/delete) the current-position expression associated with
#a window, proceed as if you are going to change the expression, but
#enter an empty expression (press the Enter key when the cursor is at
#the first character of the input field).
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#.ti -2
#\bCurrent^Position:^^Indicator\r
#.sp 1
#Here you can specify the display character to be used for the
#current-position marker (typically some kind of arrow character).
#In addition, you can specify whether that character should appear to
#the left or right of the corresponding data byte/bit in the window.
#.in -2 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#.ti -5
#\bOptions\r
#.sp 1
#Each window has certain unique, user-controllable display
#characteristics.
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#For all of the "dump" style windows, you can
#specify whether you want to see the data in both hexadecimal
#and ASCII formats, or in hexadecimal format only.
#.sp 1
#For the Register Window, you can define the fill character
#which separates a register's name from its current content (value).
#.sp 1
#For the Source Window, you can specify one of three display modes:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1)^^Code -- the software displays the window as disassembled
#instructions.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#2)^^Mixed -- the software displays the window as disassembled
#instructions, intermixed with code-generating Higher-Level Language
#(HLL) source lines, if available.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#3)^^HLL -- the software displays the window in HLL source-only mode,
#including both code-generating and non-code-generating source lines.
#Note that this display mode is also available if you are using an
#Assembler which generates line number information.
#.in -4 }
#.in -2 }
#.in -2 }
#.in -5 }
#.in -2 }
#.($60)Configure|Windows|Size
#The \bSize\r command allows you to make each window on the main screen
#larger (taller) or smaller (shorter).
#We recommend that you size the windows from top-of-screen to
#bottom-of-screen.
#The sizes of all windows \ubelow\r the one you are currently
#adjusting will change automatically in response to your adjustments.
#The sizes of all windows \uabove\r the one on which you are currently
#working will remain unchanged.
#.sp 1
#The Quick Help line at the bottom of the screen shows you which
#keys to use to affect the size changes.  If you 'Exit' without
#'Accept'ing the changes made to the last selected window,
#that window will revert to its former size.
#.($61)Configure|Windows|Goto
#The \bGoto\r command allows you to select, or "jump into",
#the windows on the main screen.
#When you do this (what we call "entering \bChange Mode\r"),
#you can browse/peruse and/or change the data in the
#windows.  Just use the cursor keys to move the highlight bar to
#the item of interest.
#To change a value, you don't even have to press the
#\bEnter\r key to pop-up the dialog box -- simply start typing
#the new value for the highlighted location/item and the
#dialogue box pops up automatically.
#.sp 1
#The Quick Help line at the bottom of the screen shows you which
#keys to use in perusing the windows.
#.sp 1
#You can find more specific information about the unique characteristics
#of Change Mode in each of the main screen windows in:
#.sp 1
#.ba {
#.in +2 {
#\l($382)Goto^|^Memory^Dump^Windows\m
#.sp 1
#\l($381)Goto^|^Register^Window\m
#.sp 1
#\l($383)Goto^|^Source^Window\m
#.sp 1
#\l($385)Goto^|^Status^Window\m
#.sp 1
#\l($384)Goto^|^Watch^Window\m
#.in -2 }
#.ea }
#.sp 1
#The host software remembers the window that you were last in
#and places you into that same window the next time you select this
#command.
#.sp 1
#\bHint:\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#As an additional, convenient shortcut to invoke
#the \bConfigure|Windows|Goto\r command,
#you can press the \bTab\r or \bShift-Tab\r keys when
#no pull-down menus are hanging off the top menu bar on the
#main screen.
#.sp 1
#Furthermore, you can use any of the following keys to do the same
#thing.  These keys differ from the \bTab\r and \bShift-Tab\r keys
#above in that they perform the action indicated by the key
#(e.g., \x19 scrolls down one line in the window's display)
#after the host software goes to the window:
#.sp 1
#.in +3 {
#.in +12 {
#.ti -12
#Keystroke^^^Effect/Action
#.ti -12
#---------^^^-------------
#.ti -12
#\x18^^^^^^^^^^^Go into window and move highlight bar or cursor
#up one line.
#.ti -12
#\x19^^^^^^^^^^^Go into window and move highlight bar or cursor
#down one line.
#.ti -12
#Home^^^^^^^^Go into window and move highlight bar or cursor to
#the beginning of the data in the window.
#.ti -12
#End^^^^^^^^^Go into window and move highlight bar or cursor to
#the end of the data in the window.
#.ti -12
#PgUp^^^^^^^^Go into window and move highlight bar or cursor
#one screenful back in the data in the window.
#.ti -12
#PgDn^^^^^^^^Go into window and move highlight bar or cursor
#one screenful ahead in the data in the window.
#.ti -12
#Ctrl-B^^^^^^Go into window and toggle (on or off) a permanent
#simple break-point at the currently highlighted location
#(Source Window only).
#.ti -12
#Ctrl-T^^^^^^Go into window and set a temporary break-point
#at the currently highlighted location in the Source Window and
#begin emulation immediately (Source Window only).
#.in -12 }
#.in -3 }
#.in -2 }
#.($62)Configure|Windows|Watch|Add
#The \bAdd\r command allows you to add a Watch \l($244)Expression\m
#to the \l($384)Watch^Window\m.
#.sp 1
#You can think of the Watch Window as one or more
#peepholes into different areas of
#memory, where you specify the starting addresses symbolically
#(the names of the variables, registers or bits) or
#numerically (expressions), and where the displayed values
#are interpreted according to the individual data types of the
#variables (if such information is available in the program file).
#.sp 1
#The Watch Window must be active before you can add anything to it.
#.sp 1
#There is no limit on the number of entities which can be
#watched -- the Watch Window is scrollable.
#.sp 1
#If the value of a Watch Expression in the Watch Window changes
#from one emulation cycle to the next
#(see \l($96)Run\m),
#that value will be highlighted to alert you of the change.
#.($63)Configure|Windows|Watch|Delete
#The \bDelete\r command allows you to delete (remove) a
#Watch \l($244)Expression\m from the \l($384)Watch^Window\m.
#.($64)Identification
#The \bIdentification\r command pops up a window
#describing the properties of your \b%rs1\r emulator system.
#The information displayed includes such things as hardware,
#firmware and software version numbers, memory sizes, model numbers
#and option configurations.
#Such information is useful, for example, when you
#call %rs2 for Technical Support at %rs3.
#.($65)Options
#The \bOptions\r command pops up a menu which allows you
#to customize miscellaneous characteristics of the user
#interface configuration.
#.($67)Alert|Diagnostics
#The \bDiagnostics\r command toggles whether or not
#your computer will beep when the host software pops up a diagnostic
#message box.  This toggle command applies to all types of
#diagnostics:  errors, warnings and informative diagnostics.
#.($68)Alert|Bad Key/Command
#The \bBad^key/command\r command toggles whether or not
#your computer will beep when you:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1)^^Press a key that is not recognized (does not apply)
#in a particular context.
#.ti -4
#2)^^Select an inactive (inaccessible) command in a pull-down
#or pop-up menu.
#.in -4 }
#.in -4 }
#.($69)Alert|Host-break
#Pressing the \bEsc\r key to break (stop) emulation is called
#"stopping emulation via a Host-break".
#.sp 1
#The \bAlert|Host-break\r command toggles whether or not
#your computer will beep when you break emulation via a "Host-break".
#(The computer will never beep when emulation stops because a normal
#hardware break-point was reached.)
#.($70)Flicker
#The \bFlicker/command\r command toggles whether or not
#the host software will flicker displayed information in
#various contexts.
#The software uses flickering as a form of
#immediate visual feedback to confirm that
#"something has happened" or "this action was taken".
#.sp 1
#Currently, the software employs flickering to show the:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1)^^selected response in a "Yes/No/Cancel" dialog.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#2)^^new value assigned to a variable or register when that
#variable or register is changed using the \l($118)Expression\m command
#\uand\r both the name and new value are typed in the
#same input box.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#3)^^selected command
#(when you have activated the \l($249)-kf\m command line option).
#.in -4 }
#.in -4 }
#.($71)Trace Prefetch
#The \bTrace^prefetch\r command toggles whether or not
#the host software will prefetch (read) raw trace buffer data from the
#\b%rs1\r emulator during keyboard "idle" time.
#.($72)Main Esc
#The \bMain^Esc\r command controls the host software's behavior
#when you press the \bEsc\r key when no pull-down menus are visible
#(pulled down) on the main screen:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +6 {
#.ti -6
#On:^^^The host software asks whether or not you really
#want to exit completely back to DOS.
#.ti -6
#Off:^^The host software treats the \bEsc\r key as an unrecognized
#keystroke and handles it according to the current setting
#of the \l($68)Alert|Bad^key/command\m toggle.
#.in -6 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#Note that the \l($121)File|Exit\m command provides an alternative
#method for exiting back to DOS.
#.($73)Function-Keys
#The \bFunction-Keys\r command displays a pull-down menu that
#gives you access to commands to change the number of
#\l($74)Lines\m of function key labels displayed and to
#\l($75)Modify\m function key assignments.
#.($74)Lines
#The \bLines\r command is used to specify the number of lines of
#\l($75)Function^Key\m labels to display at the bottom of the screen.
#Up to four lines may be used (corresponding to all function keys
#and their Shift, Alt, and Ctrl states).  Specifying 0 lines
#shuts off the display of function key labels.
#.($75)Modify Function Key Assignments
#The \bModify\r command displays a pop-up menu from which you may
#modify function key assignments.  From this menu you may
#\l($107)Assign\m new commands to function keys, \l($108)Clear\m
#function key assignments, activate (\l($111)Save\m) new
#assignments for the duration of the current session
#and \l($112)Write\m an assignment/option report.
