#.($91)File|Macro
#(Note:  The \bMacro\r command is not available in the
#COP8 Debug Module or EPU.)
#.sp 1
#The \bMacro\r command pops up the Macro Menu.
#In the Macro Menu you can create a macro command
#file or execute (run) a previously-created macro command file.
#.sp 1
#A macro command file contains a series of recorded keystrokes which,
#when executed, perform a macro function.  A macro command file may be
#used to automate long or repetitive tasks.
#.sp 1
#\bMacro Environment\r
#.sp 1
#When the emulator is in macro \l($93)learn\m mode,
#all keystrokes you type are saved to a disk file in order to be
#reproduced (executed) at a later time.  Not only are the keystrokes
#saved, but the Function Key assignments at macro-creation time are
#saved as well.  Because of this, Function Key keystrokes will perform
#the commands assigned to them at the time the macro file was created.
#If the Function Keys have been redefined, you may notice that the
#macro file Function Keys do not perform the same tasks as the
#currently defined ones.  At macro completion, the Function Keys will be
#restored to their redefined values.
#.sp 1
#Note that for macro files that may be used over a long period of time,
#it would be wise to avoid using cursor keys to select commands and to
#always use the Quick Key (the highlighted letter of the command).
#This would be useful in the situation where a host software update
#occurs where several commands may be added.  Execution of a macro
#file using cursor keys for command selection may get "out of synch",
#meaning that because of the additional new commands, commands other
#than those intended may be selected.
#.sp 1
#\bParameter Substitution\r
#.sp 1
#If the same task needs to be performed repeatedly, but values may
#change between runs, the same macro command file can be executed using
#parameters.  In general, parameters can be used anywhere the host
#software prompts you for information (filenames, numeric values,
#addresses, etc.).  When creating a macro command file, you key in a
#\b%P\r (see the \l($93)File|Macro|Learn\m command) wherever a parameter
#value is to be used.  When a macro command file is executing,
#it substitutes a parameter value whenever the \b%P\r keystroke
#combination is encountered.  You supply the actual parameter list
#when you invoke the macro command file
#(see the \l($92)File|Macro|Execute\m command).
#During macro execution, if no actual parameters are supplied and
#\b%P\r is encountered, execution pauses until you supply the actual
#parameter value.
#.($92)Macro|Execute
#The \bExecute\r command begins execution of the commands from
#a macro command file created using the
#\l($93)File|Macro|Learn\m command.
#After entering the name of a valid macro command file, you will be
#prompted for any (optional) parameters to pass to the macro command
#file.
#.sp 1
#Your should enter all actual parameter values in the order that they
#are used in the macro command file.  Pressing only \bEnter\r in
#response to a parameter prompt (without entering an actual parameter
#value) will start execution of the macro command file.
#.sp 1
#Commands are executed from the macro
#command file until the end of the file is reached or until you press
#the \bCtrl-C\r keystroke combination at the keyboard.
#Control then returns to you (manual command entry).
#.sp 1
#If you type \bCtrl-C\r while the macro is executing,
#the macro will terminate immediately and the session will
#resume in the normal, interactive mode (manual command entry).
#.sp 1
#Note that you may use the \l($249)-i\m command line option
#to start automatic execution of a macro command file when you
#invoke the host software.
#.($93)Macro|Learn
#The \bLearn\r command lets you create a macro command file by
#remembering every keystroke you type into a macro command file.
#After you enter a valid file name, control is returned to the
#Main Menu Bar and the host software enters Macro Learn Mode.
#In Learn Mode every keystroke you enter is stored in the macro
#command file until you enter
#you enter the percent character, \b%\r,
#followed by an \bE\r (\b%E\r).
#You can access all host software commands in Learn Mode except
#for the \l($91)File|Macro\m command.
#.sp 1
#\bLearn Mode Commands\r (not case sensitive)
#.sp 1
#.in +3 {
#.in +5 {
#.ti -5
#\b%E\r^^^End Macro Learn Mode.
#.sp 1
#.ti -5
#\b%%\r^^^Insert a percent sign (%) into the macro command file as
#normal text.
#.sp 1
#.ti -5
#\b%P\r^^^Parameter substitution.  Everything you type up to the next
#\bEnter\r will be passed to the program and not included in the
#macro command file.  When you press the \bEnter\r key, Learn Mode
#resumes.  During macro execution, a parameter will be pulled from the
#parameter list or, if no parameters are present, the host software
#will wait for you to supply one.
#.sp 1
#.ti -5
#\b%D\r^^^Enter a delay.  Everything you type up to the first non-digit
#character is a real-time delay (in milliseconds).  This delay is not
#the same as the global \l($94)File|Macro|Delay\m.  This delay is used
#only between the keystrokes in which it was entered in the macro
#command file.  This local delay is added to the global delay, if any.
#.sp 1
#.ti -5
#\b%N\r^^^Execute a new macro.  Everything you type up to the \bEnter\r
#is the name of a new macro file.  If you enter this during Learn Mode,
#the macro command file is closed and Learn Mode ends.
#If you enter this during execution of a macro command file,
#the current macro command file is closed, the new macro command file
#is opened and execution continues from the new file.
#.in -5 }
