#.($97)Run|Reset|Emulator
#The
#\bReset|Emulator\r
#command starts emulation from a device \bReset\r condition.
#The \bReset\r signal comes from the emulator base and occurs
#automatically.  There is no output to synchronize the target
#system, as the reset pin to the target is input only.  Some devices
#have a \bReset\r-out signal on a separate pin;  this signal,
#if present in the microcontroller itself, will be available.
#.($98)Run|Reset|Target
#The
#\bReset|Target\r
#command initiates emulation in the
#target application system, with emulation beginning from
#the chip's normal \bReset\r state.
#.sp 1
#The \b%rs1\r emulator base unit does \unot\r supply the \bReset\r
#pulse automatically.  Emulation will not begin until
#the target application system supplies the \bReset\r pulse or until
#you press the \bRESET\r button on the emulator base unit
#(if so equipped).
#.($99)Run|Go
#The \bGo\r command begins (or resumes) emulation starting from
#the current PC address.
#If you start the emulation session with a \bGo\r command from an
#unknown state (i.e., without having first \bReset\r the device),
#unpredictable behavior may result.
#.($100)Run|From
#The \bFrom\r command begins (or resumes) emulation starting from
#a program counter (PC) address that you supply.
#When you select the \bFrom\r command, the host software
#will prompt you to supply the address at which to commence emulation.
#.sp 1
#Aside from changing the program counter (PC) address, this command
#works just like the \l($99)Run|Go\m command.
#.($101)Run|Until
#The \bUntil\r command sets a temporary break-point
#which remains in effect only for the duration of the
#next emulation cycle.
#When you select the \bUntil\r command, the host software
#will prompt you to supply the temporary break-point address.
#The software then transmits this break-point to the emulator
#without disturbing any other break-points which may already exist.
#Then, emulation will begin as if you had selected the
#\l($99)Go\m command.  Emulation continues until the program
#passes through \uany\r break-point, including the temporary
#break-point just established.  When emulation stops, the
#host software removes the temporary break-point from
#the emulator, again,
#without disturbing (deleting) any other break-points which
#may have been previously established.
#.sp 1
#Aside from setting and clearing the temporary break-point, this command
#works just like the \l($99)Run|Go\m command.
#.($102)Step
#You can use the \bStep\r (single-step) command to execute
#one machine instruction and then stop (break emulation).
#The emulator will execute the next instruction
#(i.e., the one addressed by
#the Program Counter (PC)) and then break.
#.sp 1
#Note:  The single-step operation is not, strictly speaking, real-time
#emulation.  Timer-related code may not operate correctly when
#single-stepped.  This may also include edge-triggered interrupts,
#serial port functions and timer operation.
#.($103)Line
#The \bLine\r command allows you to single-step from one
#code-generating source line to the next.
#Emulation begins at the current PC and stops (breaks) when
#execution reaches the next code-generating source line in \uany\r
#module.
#.sp 1
#Note that this command will be de-activated if the loaded program
#has no line number information.
#.($104)Over
#The \bOver\r command allows you to single-step from one
#code-generating source line to the next.
#Emulation begins at the current PC and stops (breaks) when
#execution reaches the next code-generating source line in the
#\ucurrent\r module.
#This has the effect of "stepping over" calls to functions
#or procedures in other modules.
#.sp 1
#Note that this command will be de-activated if the current module
#in the loaded program has no line number information.
#.($105)To
#The \bTo\r command allows you to single-step from one
#function to the next.
#Emulation begins at the current PC and stops (breaks) when
#execution reaches the next function, procedure \uor\r
