#.($344)Mouse Support
#While \b%rs1\r currently does not provide internal, direct support
#for a mouse, you can operate \b%rs1\r with a mouse by using the
#"mouse menu" utility normally supplied by each mouse manufacturer.
#These mouse menu utilities allow you to define a mapping from
#mouse buttons/movements to keyboard keystrokes.
#.sp 1
#Regardless of which mouse you are using, mouse movement should
#always be mapped as:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.ba {
#Mouse Movement   Keystroke Mapping
#--------------   -----------------
#Left             \x1B (Left Arrow)
#Right            \x1A (Right Arrow)
#Up               \x18 (Up Arrow)
#Down             \x19 (Down Arrow)
#.ea }
#.in -4 }
#.sp 1
#You can control \b%rs1\r most effectively with a mouse if your
#mouse menu software allows you to define mouse buttons for at
#least the following keystrokes (2-button mouse):
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.ba {
#Mouse Button   Keystroke Mapping
#------------   -----------------
#Left           Enter
#       Right   Esc
#Left + Right   Tab
#.ea }
#.in -4 }
#.sp 1
#If you have a 3-button mouse, we recommend the following definitions:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.ba {
#Mouse Button            Keystroke Mapping
#---------------------   -----------------
#Left                    Enter
#                Right   Esc
#       Middle           Tab
#Left + Middle           Home
#       Middle + Right   End
#Left          + Right   Shift-Tab
#Left + Middle + Right   Ctrl-E
#.ea }
#.in -4 }
#.sp 1
#Specific details for setting up mouse menus are provided in:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
