#.($350)Description of Demo
#The ICEDEMO.EXE program (the program you are now using)
#is a demonstration version of the host PC
#software package with which you would interact to control a "real"
#%rs2 \b%rs1\r emulator.  Rather than "talking to" an emulator
#base unit, however, this demo version of the host software contains
#within it software modules which mimic (simulate) the behavior
#of a true \b%rs1\r hardware emulator base unit and
#probe card.  Also bundled in is a simulator for the %rs4
#CPU architecture.
#.sp 1
#This demo version of the host software has the following properties:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1)^^The emulator supported is an
#\b%rs1-%rs5 Model 400\r emulator
#with 32Kb of Code memory;
#however, see item 8) below for restrictions on the
#use of this memory.
#The probe card supported is that for
#a COP888CG.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#2)^^Since ICEDEMO.EXE contains within it modules which mimic
#the behavior of the emulator hardware, probe card and %rs4
#CPU, ICEDEMO.EXE consumes quite a bit more memory in the PC than the
#non-demo version of the host software.  This constrains the size
#of programs (e.g., total number of symbols) that can be loaded
#into the demo emulator.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#3)^^External events and interrupts (e.g., timer interrupts) are not
#simulated/supported.  Access to a port is treated as an access to the
#port register, not the port pins.
#Timers act as simple registers
#(i.e., they do not count automatically).  Basically, the "chip"
#simulator built into the demo emulator system simulates the %rs4
#CPU \uarchitecture\r, but does not simulate
#any \uperipheral functions\r of the
#COP888CG
#chip.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#4)^^When this "demo emulator" is placed into emulation
#(as if executing code in the
#COP888CG
#processor), the execution time reported
#will be the same as a
#10MHz COP888CG
#executing the same code.
#However, since the execution of
#each instruction is actually being simulated in software, the elapsed
#wall clock time will be significantly greater than the
#execution time displayed in the Status Window.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#5)^^The sampling rate of the demo's Performance Analyzer is much lower
#than that in an actual \b%rs1\r emulator hardware base unit.  Again,
#this is because the execution of each %rs4
#instruction is being simulated in software.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#6)^^When beginning execution from the RESET condition,
#you should select the
#\l($97)Run|Reset|Emulator\m
#command.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#7)^^The \l($356)Probe^Bit\m values captured in the Trace Buffer are
#from a simple
#7-bit
#counter.
#All other captured values (e.g., address and data) accurately reflect
#the execution flow within the simulated %rs4 CPU.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#8)^^This demo version of the emulator simulator constrains the size of
#programs which can be \l($86)File|Load\med
#or \l($115)Display/Alter|Asm/Dasm|Assemble\md into the demo emulator:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#Only
#code memory
#locations
#0000h-0055h (86 decimal bytes)
#can be loaded (initialized with code stream bytes).
#.in -4 }
#.in -4 }
#.in -4 }
