#.($394)Input Translation
#The \bInput^Translation\r command controls the translation/conversion
#applied to keyboard input and to symbols read from a
#program file (\l($86)File|Load\m).
#The host software performs this conversion before any
#subsequent processing (e.g., inserting a symbol into the symbol
#table or searching the symbol table for a specific symbol).
#The possible choices are:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1.^^\l($395)As^Is\m:^^No conversion.
#.ti -4
#2.^^\l($396)Lower\m:^^Convert to lower case.
#.ti -4
#3.^^\l($397)Upper\m:^^Convert to upper case.
#.in -4 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#.($395)Input Translation|As Is
#Keyboard input and symbols read from a program file are not
#converted/translated at all.
#If your program contains symbols which differ from each other
#only in case (e.g., 'InnerLoop' and 'innerloop') you must select this
#option to be able to reference both symbols.
#.($396)Input Translation|Lower
#The host software converts keyboard input and symbols read from a
#program file to lower case before any subsequent processing.
#.($397)Input Translation|Upper
#The host software converts keyboard input and symbols read from a
#program file to upper case before any subsequent processing.
#.($398)Using Virtual Memory
#By default, virtual heap memory is enabled.  This allows the host
#software to use memory located outside the conventional 0-640Kb memory
#area normally usable by a DOS program.  This capability is useful
#if you have a program with a large number of symbols which cannot
#all fit into the symbol table in conventional memory.
#.sp 1
#If you ever see the following message
#at the top of the screen
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#"Not enough unused memory available to continue ... press any key."
#.in -4 }
#.sp 1
#the host software has used up all the
#conventional memory and, generally, cannot continue.
#This may mean that you do not have Expanded Memory in your system.
#If this happens, you should verify that you do have Expanded Memory
#available. If you have only a small amount of Expanded Memory
#available you may want to
#reinvoke the host software, specifying the
#command line options (\l($249)-vmf^and^-vmfn\m)
#to enable virtual heap memory usage on disk.
#.sp 1
#Note that loading files (building the internal symbol
#table) and some other commands within the host software
#(e.g., listing all symbols alphabetically) may be much slower when
#virtual heap memory is enabled.
#.sp 1
#There are two basic kinds of backing store media (real memory/storage)
#used to support the virtual heap memory:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1.^^LIM EMS 3.2/4.0 Expanded Memory
#.ti -4
#2.^^a file
#.in -4 }
#.in -4 }
#.sp 1
#The fastest medium is expanded memory and you should use this form if:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1.^^You have an expanded memory card in your computer
#(any type of PC).  To allow access to this expanded memory,
#you must have the appropriate 'DEVICE=..' statement in your
#CONFIG.SYS file;  see the documentation accompanying your
#expanded memory board for the details on how to do this.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#or
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#2.^^You have a '386 or '486 PC with no expanded memory,
#but with additional memory (extended memory) above the
#1Mb boundary.  In such a case, you can use the EMM386.EXE
#device driver supplied with DOS to simulate expanded memory
#in extended memory.  A typical CONFIG.SYS file for this
#configuration would contain the following lines:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.ba {
#DEVICE=C:\DOS50\HIMEM.SYS
#DEVICE=C:\DOS50\EMM386.EXE
#.ea }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#You can add additional parameters to the DEVICE command
#for EMM386.EXE to control the amount of expanded memory
#to be created (simulated) in your system.
#.in -4 }
#.in -4 }
#.sp 1
#If your computer does not fit either of the two categories above,
#but it does have extended memory (memory beyond the 1Mb boundary),
#you can use a file for virtual heap backing store.
#However, rather than using a file on your hard disk,
#create a RAM disk in extended memory and, using the \l($249)-vmfn\m
#command line option, designate the backing store file as a file
#on that RAM disk drive.  This is tremendously faster than using an
#actual hard disk file.
#.($399)Description of Simulator
#The ICESIM.EXE program (the program you are now using)
#is a simulation version of the host PC
#software package with which you would interact to control a "real"
#%rs2 \b%rs1\r emulator.  Rather than "talking to" an emulator base
#unit, however, this simulator version of the host software contains
#within it software modules which mimic (simulate) the behavior
#of a true \b%rs1\r hardware emulator base unit and
#probe card.  Also bundled in is a simulator for the %rs4
#CPU architecture.
#.sp 1
#This simulator version of the host software has
#the following properties:
#.sp 1
#.in +4 {
#.in +4 {
#.ti -4
#1)^^The emulator supported is an
#\b%rs1-%rs5 Model 400\r emulator
#with 32Kb of Code memory.
#Any %rs4 based $MODEL file may be generated and used.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#2)^^Since ICESIM.EXE contains within it modules which mimic
#the behavior of the emulator hardware, probe card and %rs4
#CPU, ICESIM.EXE consumes quite a bit more memory in the PC than the
#non-simulator version of the host software.  This constrains the size
#of programs (e.g., total number of symbols) that can be loaded
#into the demo emulator.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#3)^^External events and interrupts (e.g., timer interrupts) are not
#simulated/supported.  Access to a port is treated as an access to the
#port register, not the port pins.
#Timers act as simple registers
#(i.e., they do not count automatically).  Basically, the "chip"
#simulator built into the demo emulator system simulates the %rs4
#CPU \uarchitecture\r, but does not simulate
#any \uperipheral functions\r of the %rs4
#family devices.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#4)^^When this simulator is placed into emulation (executing code
#in the simulated
#processor), the execution time reported
#will be the same as a
#10MHz COP888CG
#executing the same code.
#However, since the execution of
#each instruction is actually being simulated in software, the elapsed
#wall clock time will be significantly greater than the
#execution time displayed in the Status Window.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#5)^^The sampling rate of the simulator's Performance Analyzer is much
#lower than that in an actual \b%rs1\r emulator hardware base unit.
#Again, this is because the execution of each %rs4
#instruction is being simulated in software.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#6)^^When beginning execution from the RESET condition,
#you should select the
#\l($97)Run|Reset|Emulator\m
#command.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#7)^^The \l($356)Probe^Bit\m values captured in the Trace Buffer are
#randomly-generated values.
#All other captured values (e.g., address and data) accurately reflect
#the execution flow within the simulated CPU.
#.sp 1
#.ti -4
#.in -4 }
#.in -4 }
