#.($435)File|Unload
#The \bUnload\r command allows you to 'unload' a program (and clean
#up the current application environment).  The command initializes
#code
#memory, symbol and HLL information (except variables
#from the $MODEL file), watch expressions, breakpoints, mapping and
#trace settings.
#.($436)EPROM Device Selection
#The \bDevice\r command allows you to select the
#EPROM device to be programmed from the list of
#supported devices. Note that the package types
#are listed in parenthesis next to the devices.
#.($437)EPROM Configuration Options Pulldown
#The \bConfiguration\r pulldown is available for you
#to choose which Configuration Options to program into the
#\l($454)ECON^Register\m or the configuration byte at 0x1FFF
#(depending on which \l($436)EPROM^Device\m is selected).
#The available Options are \l($438)Security\m,
#\l($441)Clock^Type\m, \l($445)RAM^Size\m and
#\l($537)Halt\m.
#.($438)EPROM Security Options
#From the \bSecurity\r pulldown menu you may \l($439)Enable\m
#or \l($440)Disable\m EPROM security.  The default state of the
#security feature is \bdisabled\r.
#.($439)Enable EPROM Security
#The \bEnable\r command is used to program (set to 0) the
#EPROM security bit in the \l($454)ECON^Register\m.  When
#security is enabled, reads and writes of the EPROM are
#disabled.  If a read is attempted all memory addresses
#will read as 0E0 hex.
#.($440)Disable EPROM Security
#The \bDisable\r command is used to leave the EPROM security
#bit in the \l($454)ECON^Register\m unprogrammed (1).  When
#security is disabled, reads and writes of the EPROM are enabled.
#Security Disabled is the default condition (since it
#corresponds to the unprogrammed value of the security bit).
#.($441)Clock Options
#From the \bClock\r pulldown menu you may select the type of
#clock your device will use.  Available options are
#\l($442)External\m, \l($443)RC^Oscillator\m and
#\l($444)Crystal^Oscillator\m.  The default Clock type
#is \bExternal\r.
#.($442)External Clock Option
#The \bExternal\r command is used to select the External
#Clock Option by leaving the clock option
#bits in the \l($454)ECON^Register\m unprogrammed (1,1).
#The External Clock is the default condition (since it
#corresponds to the unprogrammed value of the clock bits).
#.($443)RC Oscillator Clock Option
#The \bRC^Oscillator\r command is used to select the
#RC Oscillator Clock Option by programming (to 0)
#bit 3 and leaving unprogrammed (1) bit 4 in the
#\l($454)ECON^Register\m.
#.($444)Crystal Oscillator Clock Option
#The \bCrystal^Oscillator\r command is used to select the
#Crystal Oscillator Clock Option by programming (to 0)
#bits 3 and 4 in the \l($454)ECON^Register\m.
#.($445)RAM Size Options
#From the \bRAM^Size\r pulldown menu you may select the RAM Size
#for the device you are going to emulate.  Available options are
#\l($446)128^Bytes\m and \l($447)64^Bytes\m.
#The default RAM Size is 128 Bytes.
#.($446)128 Byte RAM Size Option
#The \b128^Byte\r command is used to select the 128 Byte Ram
#Size Option by leaving unprogrammed (1) bit 1 in the
#\l($454)ECON^Register\m or programming 0x6F into the
#configuration byte at 0x1FFF for 28-Pin COP880 devices
#(depending on which \l($436)EPROM^Device\m is selected).
#The 128 Byte option is used to
#emulate COP840 and COP880 devices.  128 bytes is the default
#condition.
#.($447)64 Byte RAM Size Option
#The \b64^Byte\r command is used to select the 64 Byte Ram
#Size Option by programming (to 0) bit 1 in the
#\l($454)ECON^Register\m or programming 0xEF into the
#configuration byte at 0x1FFF for 28-Pin COP880 devices
#(depending on which \l($436)EPROM^Device\m is selected).
#The 64 Byte option is used to emulate COP820 devices.
#.($448)Auto
#The \bAuto\r command is available in the
#\l($462)Programmer^Menu\m as a one step method for
#programming EPROMs.  The sequence that is followed for
#COP8780 family devices is:
#.sp 1
#.ba {
#   1 - \bVerify\r the \l($454)Signature^Byte\m.
#   2 - \l($456)Blank^Check\m the ECON Register.
#   3 - \l($451)Blank^Check\m the EPROM.
#   4 - \l($452)Program\m the EPROM.
#   5 - \l($453)Verify\m the EPROM.
#   6 - \l($457)Program\m the ECON Register.
#   7 - \l($458)Verify\m the ECON Register.
#   8 - If security enabled Verify the EPROM reads as 0xE0.
#.ea }
#.sp 1
#The sequence followed for the COP8 HYBRID devices is:
#.sp 1
#.ba {
#   1 - \l($451)Blank^Check\m the EPROM.
#   2 - \l($452)Program\m the EPROM.
#   3 - \l($453)Verify\m the EPROM.
#.ea }
#.sp 1
#The address range for EPROM commands defaults initially to the
#EPROM size for the selected device but may be changed using the
#\l($460)Range\m command.
#Note that each of the steps listed above is available
#as a separate command and may be performed manually.
#.($449)EPROM Programming Commands
#From the \bEPROM\r pulldown menu you may select commands
#to work with the EPROM.  Available commands are
#\l($450)Read\m, \l($451)Blank^Check\m, \l($452)Program\m
#and \l($453)Verify\m.
#.($450)Read EPROM
#The \bRead\r command is used to read data from an EPROM
#into the Debug Module's code memory. The code memory may be
#perused, modified and written to a file using the commands in the
#\l($459)Code\m pulldown menu in the \l($462)Programmer^Menu\m.
#The address range for EPROM commands defaults initially to the
#EPROM size for the selected device but may be changed using the
#\l($460)Range\m command.
#.($451)Blank Check EPROM
#The \bBlank^Check\r command is used to blank check the EPROM.
#The unprogrammed (blank) value of a byte is FF hex.
#The address range for EPROM commands defaults initially to the
#EPROM size for the selected device but may be changed using the
#\l($460)Range\m command.
#You can find more information about EPROM
#programming in the \l$(462)Programmer^Menu\m.
#.($452)Program EPROM
#The \bProgram\r command is used to program data into the EPROM.
#The data to be programmed should be loaded into the Debug
#Module's code memory.  Note that each byte that is programmed
#is verified after it is programmed so it is not
#strictly necessary to verify the device after programming.
#You can find more information about EPROM
#programming in the \l$(462)Programmer^Menu\m.
#The address range for EPROM commands defaults initially to the
#EPROM size for the selected device but may be changed using the
#\l($460)Range\m command.
#.sp 1
#Note for all MCP devices that the default address range
#is 0x0000-0x7FFF (32Kb).
#.($453)Verify EPROM
#The \bVerify\r command is used to verify the data in the EPROM.
#The data in the EPROM is compared against the data in the
#Debug Module's code memory.
#You can find more information about EPROM
#programming in the \l$(462)Programmer^Menu\m.
#The address range for EPROM commands defaults initially to the
#EPROM size for the selected device but may be changed using the
#\l($460)Range\m command.
#.($454)EPROM Register Programming Commands
#The \bRegister\r pulldown menu is available if the
#currently selected device is in the COP8780 family.
#From this menu you may select commands to work with
#the \bECON\r register and the \bSignature\r byte.
#The available commands are
#\l($455)Read\m, \l($456)Blank^Check\m, \l($457)Program\m
#and \l($458)Verify\m.
#.($455)Read ECON and Signature Byte
#The \bRead\r command is used to read the value of the
#ECON register and the signature byte.
#You can find more information about EPROM
#programming in the \l$(462)Programmer^Menu\m.
#.($456)Blank Check ECON
#The \bBlank^Check\r command is used to blank check the value
#of the ECON register and to verify that the signature byte
#is E0 hex.  The unprogrammed value of the ECON register
#is 7F hex on OTP parts and FF hex on windowed parts.
#You can find more information about EPROM
#programming in the \l$(462)Programmer^Menu\m.
#.($457)Program ECON
#The \bProgram\r command is used to program the ECON
#register.  The value used corresponds to the options
#set through the \l($437)Configuration\m pulldown menu.
#You can find more information about EPROM
#programming in the \l$(462)Programmer^Menu\m.
#.($458)Verify ECON and Signature byte
#The \bVerify\r command is used to verify the value of the ECON
#register and the signature byte.  The expected value of the
#ECON register corresponds to the options
#set through the \l($437)Configuration\m pulldown menu.
#You can find more information about EPROM
#programming in the \l$(462)Programmer^Menu\m.
#.($459)Code Memory Utilities
#From the \bCode\r pulldown menu you may select commands
#to \l($86)Load\m a file, patch code (\l($113)Assembly\m
#or \l($252)Hex\m), set the \l($460)Code^Range\m
#over which EPROM commands apply and perform a
#\l($468)Checksum\m on the emulator's code memory.
#.($460)EPROM Programming Code Range
#The Code \bRange\r command is used to set the starting and
#ending address range that will be used for all EPROM
#commands.  When the \l($462)Programmer^Menu\m is entered
#and when a new device is chosen the address range is
#initialized to the EPROM size (0 - 0x0FFF for the COP8780
#family and 0 - 0x1FFF for COP8 HYBRID devices).
#.sp 1
#Note for all MCP devices that the default address range
#is 0x0000-0x7FFF (32Kb).
#.($462)Programmer Menu
#The COP8 Debug Module can program the code memory in any of
#the supported EPROM (or HYBRID) \l($436)devices\m in any
#package style.  All \l($449)programming\m operations use the code
#memory in the Debug Module.  The code memory may be
#loaded, viewed and patched from the \l($459)Programmer|Code\m
#pulldown menu.  The code range for all operations is
#initially over the entire code space but may be modified using
#the \l($460)Code|Range\m command.
#.sp 1
#You must supply the VPP voltage for all
#programming operations.  The voltage level
#at the VPP connector on the Debug Module
#must be between 12.7V and 13.3V.
#Both VPP and VCC for the device to be programmed are derived
#from the VPP supplied at the terminal block so the power
#supply used must source at least 100mA of current at the
#specified voltage.  Failure to supply the correct and
#adequate power to the VPP terminal will result in erratic
#or possibly destructive programming.  The rest of the
#Debug Module is powered from the terminal block VCC, which
#must be 5V +/- 5%.
#.sp 1
#Make sure that only one device is in any one of the three
#ZIF sockets at a time.  Do not insert a device into any of
#the sockets until prompted to do so by the Host Software,
#and always remove the device (when prompted to do so)
#before leaving the Programmer Menu or the device may be
#damaged.
#.($464)Init Code Memory
#The \bInit^Code^Memory\r
#command is used to define
#the value that will be used to initialize all of the emulator's
