#This file can be used later to write to external
#data memory using the \l($89)Download\m command.
#When you select the \bHex\r command, a dialog box will pop up to
#prompt you for the address range of the block to write and
#for the name of the file to write to.
#.($467)S-Record
#You can use the \bS-Record\r command to write external data memory
#to a reloadable disk file in \bMotorola^S-Record\r format.
#This file can be used later to write to external
#data memory using the \l($89)Download\m command.
#When you select the \bS-Record\r command, a dialog box will pop up to
#prompt you for the address range of the block to write and
#for the name of the file to write to.
#.($468)Checksum
#The \bChecksum\r command is available to perform a
#checksum on the emulator's code memory (for the selected
#\l($460)Code^Range\m).
#.($471)Radix
#The \bRadix\r command allows you to specify the default radix (base)
#for numbers typed at the keyboard.
#Note that you can override the \bRadix\r
#command, on a case-by-case basis, by supplying an explicit base
#designator (e.g., 101y, 707o, 456., 0x123 or 123h) as
#explained in \l$(244)Expression^Input\m.
#.($472)Radix -- Binary
#The \bBinary\r command sets the default radix (base) for numbers
#typed at the keyboard to binary (base 2).
#.($473)Radix -- Octal
#The \bOctal\r command sets the default radix (base) for numbers
#typed at the keyboard to octal (base 8).
#.($474)Radix -- Decimal
#The \bDecimal\r command sets the default radix (base) for numbers
#typed at the keyboard to decimal (base 10).
#.($475)Radix -- Hexadecimal
#The \bHexadecimal\r command sets the default radix (base) for numbers
#typed at the keyboard to hexadecimal (base 16).
#.($476)Browse Window
#The \bBrowse^Window\r is used to inspect or change structures,
#unions, arrays, bit-fields and pointers.
#The Browse Window is entered by pressing \bCtrl-B\r from the
#\l($140)Global\m, \l($141)Local\m, \l($142)Alpha\m and
#\l($143)Address\m symbol displays, the \l($384)Watch^Window\m,
#the \l($118)Display/Alter|Expression\m box and from the
#Browse Window.  The Browse Window is available
#when the \bCtrl-B:Browse\r prompt is displayed.
#Press \bEsc\r to exit the current Browse Window.
#.sp 1
#For each element of the object being browsed
#the address, data type and value are
#displayed.  In addition, \l($481)Array^Subscript^Values\m
#and \l($480)Member^Names\m may be displayed where appropriate.
#Note that the indentation level for values of members of
#structures and unions, which are themselves structures or unions,
#may be controlled
#using the \l($482)Struct/Union^Indentation\m command.
#.sp 1
#Note also that calls to the Browse Window may be stacked by
#toggling \l($478)Browse^Window^Stack\m to 'ON'.
#.($477)Expressions
#The \bExpressions\r command calls a pulldown menu
#from which you may the control input options for
#\l($394)Input^Translation\m, \l($471)Default^Radix\m and
#display options for \l($270)Unknown^data^type^size\m,
#\l($478)Browse^Window^Stacking\m,
#\l($482)Structure/Union^Indentation\m, \l($480)Member^Names\m,
#\l($481)Array^Subscript^Values\m and \l($479)Value^Formats\m.
#.sp 1
#Also see \l($244)Expression^Input^Syntax\m.
#.sp 1
#The following section describes display formatting conventions
#used by the iceMASTER Host Software.
#.sp 1
#\bAddress Display Format\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#Addresses displayed in the
#\l($140)Global\m, \l($141)Local\m, \l($142)Alpha\m and
#\l($143)Address\m symbol displays, the \l($384)Watch^Window\m,
#the \l($476)Browse^Window\m, the \l($118)Display/Alter|Expression\m
#box and the \l($118)Change^Box\m are of the form:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#\um:hhhh\r
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#where \um\r is a character denoting the memory space and \uhhhh\r is
#the hexadecimal offset into that memory space.  The possible values
#for \um\r are described in \l($574)Memory^Space^Constants\m.
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\bFundamental Type Display Format\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#The type names displayed for symbols which are fundamental
#types are described in \l($573)Fundamental^Type^Names\m.
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\bBit Field Type Display Format\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#The type name displayed for structure members which are
#C bit fields shows the underlying integral type preceded by the
#bit offset into an object of that type and the number of bits
#in the field.  The general format is:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#\uo:l_type-name\r
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#where \uo\r is the offset to the least significant bit of the field,
#\ul\r is the field length (in bits) and \utype-name\r is the name
#of the underlying type.  The offset \uo\r is zero-relative, starting
#at the least significant bit.  For example, the bits in a \bchar\r
#are numbered 7,..,0, with 7 being the most significant bit and 0
#being the least significant bit.
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\bDerived/Aggregate Type Display Format\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#The general forms follow, where "..." represents any data type name.
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.ba {
#Display Format   Meaning
#---------------  ----------------------------------
#->...            "pointer to ..."
#[n]...           "array of n ..."
#()...            "function returning ..."
#struct           (structure with no tag-name)
#struct \utag-name\r  (structure with tag-name \utag-name\r)
#union            (union with no tag-name)
#union \utag-name\r   (union with tag-name \utag-name\r)
#.ea }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#Note, in the examples that follow, that data type names displayed
#by the Host Software read strictly from left-to-right, unlike C,
#where data types read "from the inside out".
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.ba {
#                Example
#Display Format  C Declaration      Data Type
#--------------  -----------------  ----------------------------
#->int           int*  pi;          pointer to int
#[5]int          int   x[5];        array of 5 int's
#()int           int   f();         function returning int
#[15]->char      char*  y[15];      array of 15 pointers to char
#->()->[8]->int  int* (*(*z)())[8]  pointer to a function
#                                   returning a pointer to an
#                                   array of 8 pointers to int
#.ea }
#.in -2 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\bFundamental Type Value Format\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#Fundamental types can be displayed in three formats:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.ba {
#- Character (enclosed in apostrophes('))
#- Decimal (signed or unsigned)
#- Hexadecimal
#.ea }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#You can control the display format for a fundamental data type using
#the \l($479)Value^Formats\m options.
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\bPointer Value Formats\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#The display format of a pointer value depends on the "nature"
#of the pointer.
#.sp 1
#\uGeneric Pointers\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#Generic pointers are needed due to the Harvard architecture of the
#8051.  A generic pointer occupies three bytes in memory.  The first
#byte contains a selector value/code denoting which memory space is
#being referenced.  The next two bytes contain the offset (0-0xFFFF)
#into that memory space.
#.sp 1
#A generic pointer is used when the compiler does not know (at
#compile-time) what memory space a pointer variable may reference.
#.sp 1
#The general display format for a generic pointer is:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#\um:hhhh\r
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#where \um\r is a character denoting the memory space and \uhhhh\r
#is the hexadecimal offset into that memory space.  The possible
#memory space characters (\um\r) are listed in the table in
#the description of the \bAddress Display Format\r (above).
#.sp 1
#The display format for invalid (illegal, erroneous) generic
#pointers is:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#\u?:hhhh{sshhhh}
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#An invalid generic pointer value is one with an illegal memory
#space selector byte value.  This can occur for several reasons:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#1) The application program has not yet initialized (assigned) the
#pointer.
#.sp 1
#2) The application program has erroneously "clobbered" (written over)
#the the memory space selector byte value in the pointer.
#.sp 1
#3) The linker has assigned (static) variables to memory locations so
#that local variables in functions which cannot be active at the same
#time share the same memory for their local variables.  For example,
#if functions 'a' and 'b' can never be active at the same time, the
#linker may assign the local variables in function 'a' such that they
#use the same memory locations as the local variables in 'b'.
#If function 'b' is currently active and you display a local pointer
#defined in function 'a', the value in that pointer variable may appear
#to be "bad".
#.in -2 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\uMemory-Specific Pointers\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#Memory-specific pointers are used when the compiler knows (at
#compile-time) what memory space is being referenced by a
#particular pointer.  Memory-specific pointers occupy only the
#number of bytes required to hold an address spanning that
#particular memory space.  Memory-specific pointers do not
#contain a memory space selector byte.
#.sp 1
#The general display formats for memory-specific pointers are:
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#.ba {
#\u(m):hh\r
#\u(m):hhhh\r
#.ea }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#where \um\r, \uhh\r and \uhhhh\r are the same as for generic
#pointers.  The memory space designator \um\r is included to let
#you know which memory space is being referenced by the offset
#value \uhh\r or \uhhhh\r.  It is enclosed in parentheses to let
#you know that the memory space is implicit (i.e., there is no
#memory space selector byte stored in memory).
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\uNULL Pointers\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#The display format for a NULL pointer value is "(null)".  The
#rules for detecting a NULL pointer depend on the compiler you
#are using.
#.in -2 }
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\bStructure, Union and Array Value Formats\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#The single-line "value" displayed for a structure, union or array
#object is the data type name itself ("struct..", "union.." or
#"[n]...").
#.sp 1
#In the main-screen Watch Window and the pop-up Browse Window,
#structures, unions and arrays are fully-expanded to show the
#individual members or elements.  In these contexts, the
#individual members in a struct/union and the individual elements
#in an array immediately follow the single-line header for the
#structure, union or array.  Each of these lines shows the address,
#data type, and value of the struct/union member or array element.
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#\bFunctions\r
#.sp 1
#.in +2 {
#The value displayed for an object of type "function returning ..."
#is the name of the function itself.  This may seem redundant in the
#simple case, but if you are dereferencing (indirecting through /
#following) a pointer to a function, the Host Software displays
#the name of the function pointed to by the pointer.
#.in -2 }
#.sp 1
#.($478)Browse Window Stack
#The \bBrowse^Window^Stack\r command is used to control how the
#system behaves when you are in the \l($476)Browse^Window\m and
#press \bCtrl-B\r to browse another structure, union, array or
#pointer.
#.sp 1
#When 'ON' the Host Software will 'stack up' calls to the Browse
#Window.  In this case when you press \bEsc\r to leave the Browse
#Window you will backup to the previous Browse Window.  This is
#not the default behavior because each window saved consumes
#memory on the runtime stack, which may overflow if you
#traverse too deeply into a linked list or tree.
#.sp 1
#When 'OFF' the Host Software closes the current Browse Window
#before opening the next one.  The net effect is that you can
#follow a pointer, but cannot backup to the previous window by
#pressing the \bEsc\r key.  When you do press the \bEsc\r key
#you will exit the Browse Window and return to the point where the
#Browse Window was first invoked.  This is the \udefault\r behavior.
#.sp 1
#Note that when 'OFF' you can keep track of the nodes/objects you
#have visited while browsing by pressing \bCtrl-W\r to write to a
#file which may be perused later.
#.($479)Value Formats
#The \bValue^Formats\r options are available so that you may
#choose the format in which to display program variables in the
#\l($140)Global\m, \l($141)Local\m, \l($142)Alpha\m and
#\l($143)Address\m symbol displays, the \l($384)Watch^Window\m, the
#\l($118)Display/Alter|Expression\m box, the
#\l($118)Change^Box\m and the \l($476)Browse^Window\m.
#.sp 1
#Values for char data type variables are always displayed in
#hexadecimal, decimal and as an ASCII character so there is no
#format option for the char data type.  All other fundamental data
#types can be displayed in decimal, hexadecimal or ASCII.
#The default display format selections
#are decimal for short, integer, long, float and double data types
#and hexadecimal for unknown data types.
#Note that up to 4 bytes can be displayed for an unknown data type
#(see the
#\l($270)Unknown^Data^Type^Size\m command).
#.($480)Member Name
#The \bMember Name\r command is a toggle, used to control
#the display of structure or union member names in the
#\l($476)Browse^Window\m.  If 'ON' (which is the default) the member
#names are displayed and if 'OFF' the member names are not displayed.
#Note that when \l($480)Member^Name\m is 'OFF' the value chosen for the
#\l($482)Structure/Union^Indentation\m command is unimportant; the
#Browse Window will look the same.
#.($481)Array Subscript Values
#The \bArray^Subscript^Values\r command is a toggle, used to control
#the display of array subscript values in the \l($476)Browse^Window\m.
#If 'ON' (which is the default) the array subscript values are
#displayed and if 'OFF' the array subscript values are not displayed.
#In either case, the value of the array at that subscript is displayed.
#.($482)Structure/Union Indentation
#The \bStructure/Union^Indentation\r command is available to
#control indentation of structure/union
#members (which are themselves structures, unions or arrays)
#when in the \l($476)Browse^Window\m.  There are 2 types
#of indentation available: \l($483)Long\m and \l($484)Short\m (default).
#Note that when \l($480)Member^Name\m is 'OFF' the value chosen for the
#\bStructure/Union^Indentation\r command is unimportant; the
#Browse Window will look the same.
#.($483)Long
#The \bLong\r command sets the "long" style of indentation for
#structures, unions or arrays in the \l($476)Browse^Window\m.
#The "long" indentation style means that members of a
#structure, union or array (which is part of the structure, union
#or array that is being "browsed") are indented 2 spaces past the longest
#member name.
#.($484)Short
#The \bShort\r command sets the "short" style of indentation for
#structures, unions or arrays in the \l($476)Browse^Window\m.
#The "short" indentation style means that members of a
#structure, union or array (which is part of the structure, union
#or array that is being "browsed") are indented just 2 spaces past the
#the start of the member name field.  This is the default indentation
#style.
#.($485)Configure|Windows|Watch
